From 9ffcd9e90ac5fecb6904830be1d7935fcfbc8f9f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Victor <victor@local>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 04:30:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] tp: 0018a attention-shard equivalence tests (kda/width check)

Adds the two equivalence harnesses written during the 0019 correctness
investigation and registers them as build targets:

  runtime/examples/kimi_k3_tp_kda_check.cpp    KDA/MLA head sharding
  runtime/examples/kimi_k3_tp_width_check.cpp  MoE FFN width sharding

Both answer the same question from opposite ends of the shard: does splitting
the work across ranks and recombining it reproduce the unsharded result to
float-reassociation tolerance? That is the property 0018's SHARD_KDA/_MLA kill
switches exist to A/B, and these are what turned "MLA sharding looks fine" into
a measured relL2 8.2e-09 at the first MLA layer.

They were written on the fleet but never captured. The sources have been sitting
in the recipe at patches/sparkinfer/next/new-tests/ with nothing to install them,
and no patch ever added their add_executable lines -- so they were unbuildable
from a clean checkout and the CMakeLists.txt hunks in 0021/0024/0025 carry
kimi_k3_tp_kda_check and kimi_k3_tp_width_check as unchanged *context*, which is
why 0021 fails on runtime/CMakeLists.txt even where its source hunks apply.

Placement is derived, not guessed: 0021's hunk header is `@@ -181,6 +181,10 @@`
with `target_link_libraries(kimi_k3_tp_kda_check ...)` as its first context line,
which pins the block to exactly this position -- immediately before
kimi_k3_tp_phase_check -- and after this commit line 181 is that line verbatim.
Nothing between 0007 and 0021 otherwise touches runtime/CMakeLists.txt, so the
numbering is stable across the rest of the series.
